On Tue, 9 Jan 2001, George Cora wrote:

>We've been looking at (but yet to experiment with) the Security-Enhanced
>Linux distribution, which the NSA recently published. We were somewhat
>surprised that this distribution does not _appear_ to have any extended 
>logging or auditing functions, in the same way as other C2 compliant O/S
>have, such as Win NT Event Logging, Solaris BSM, AIX, etc. Is planned to
>have a audit module included in "selinux"?

As mentioned on the web page, this work is not intended as a complete
security solution for Linux.  Security-Enhanced Linux is an implementation
of a flexible mandatory access control architecture that can support
a wide range of security policies.  It does not currently address other 
important security features such as security auditing.  

Although the focus of the work has not been on security auditing,
the architecture does provide some simple support for selective
auditing of access grantings and/or denials based on the security
policy configuration.  However, the audit data generated by the mandatory
access controls is currently handled by the existing kernel logging
support, which we recognize as being insufficient for auditing.
The mandatory access controls also provide a foundation for protecting
audit logs and audit-related applications.  Likewise, the subject and
object labeling support provides a basis for meaningful audit
information.

We are interested in the development of a more general audit
framework, in the development of a real kernel audit subsystem,
and in the development of audit analysis tools that make use of
the mandatory access controls.  However, we are currently focused
on work related to the mandatory access control architecture.
We hope to leverage the work of others in the area of auditing
at some point.  

